\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \New  Mass scan on $24^3\times 48$, one random right-hand side, means over the four configurations. CGNE and the multigrid (GCR(2) on $\tilde  A_{L}$) both stop at $|D\psi -\eta |^2/|\eta |^2<10^{-18}$. Neff's $\alpha $ is $0.6$ for both; the multigrid is also run at $\alpha =1$. \New  CGNE runs in mixed precision. Columns: iterations, solve seconds, and coarse-operator applications. $N_{\rm  rhs}=t_{\rm  setup}/(t_{\rm  CGNE}-t_{\rm  MG})$ is the number of right-hand sides at which the multigrid setup is paid back; \New  a dash in that column means that the multigrid solve is not faster than CGNE. Speed-up: the solve time of CGNE over that of the multigrid, smallest and largest over the four configurations.}}{28}{table.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:massscan}{{5}{28}{\New Mass scan on $24^3\times 48$, one random right-hand side, means over the four configurations. CGNE and the multigrid (GCR(2) on $\AL $) both stop at $|D\psi -\eta |^2/|\eta |^2<10^{-18}$. Neff's $\alpha $ is $0.6$ for both; the multigrid is also run at $\alpha =1$. \New CGNE runs in mixed precision. Columns: iterations, solve seconds, and coarse-operator applications. $N_{\rm rhs}=t_{\rm setup}/(t_{\rm CGNE}-t_{\rm MG})$ is the number of right-hand sides at which the multigrid setup is paid back; \New a dash in that column means that the multigrid solve is not faster than CGNE. Speed-up: the solve time of CGNE over that of the multigrid, smallest and largest over the four configurations}{table.5}{}}
